WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.050
FOOD DELIVERY:
Food delivery apps are a scam.

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.779
Do you want to get Chinese food?

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:05.779
Uber Eats.

00:00:05.779 --> 00:00:07.250
Do you want to get Indian food?

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:08.250
Uber Eats.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.250
Do you want to get burgers?

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.250
Uber Eats.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.000
Do you want to get pizza?

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.620
Well, maybe DoorDash.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:19.200
Food delivery has become a ubiquitous service
that virtually everyone uses.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.920
In fact, 86% of Americans use food delivery
atleast once a month.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.460
31% use atleast twice a week.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.099
And as for millennials, I mean, this is basically
their entire diet.

00:00:32.099 --> 00:00:38.160
This is not too surprising given the extraordinary
convenience of food delivery apps.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.480
You can order from virtually any restaurant
in your vicinity and have it delivered to

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.790
your doorstep within an hour.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:49.680
This is in stark contrast to food delivery
just 10 years ago when your options were basically

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.370
just limited to pizza.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:56.230
This unprecedented surge has allowed for the
food delivery industry to thrive with several

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.640
apps popping up from Uber Eats and DoorDash
to GrubHub and Postmates.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:07.189
But, while these apps sure are convenient,
they’re not exactly a great deal for anyone.

00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:14.010
The average American spends $37.28 every week
on food delivery which translates to $150

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.870
per month and over $1800 per year.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:22.400
And over a third of that amount is simply
going towards service fees, delivery fees,

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.570
and tips coming in at $13 per order or $650
per year.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:32.040
But, I’m sure you already knew that.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.950
For most people, food delivery is more of
a guilty pleasure than anything.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:42.130
But, unlike Netflix binging and singing in
your car, this habit will cost a lot of young

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.130
adults their retirement.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.180
In fact, if young adults simply invested food
delivery money into the S&amp;P 500 instead between

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:54.680
the ages of 20 and 65, they would end up with
an extra $715,000 instead of a bunch of empty

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.230
Panda Express boxes.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:01.320
But, it’s not just end consumers who are
getting a bad bargain either.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:07.000
Food delivery is also not that great for restaurants,
delivery drivers, or even the food delivery

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.200
apps themselves.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.640
Yet, the popularity of food delivery is only
continuing to skyrocket.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.500
So, here’s why food delivery apps are kind
of a scam for everyone and why no one seems

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.599
to care anyway.

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:29.780
SCAMMING CONSUMERS:
We all know that food delivery is expensive,

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.360
but just how expensive is it?

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.569
Well, let’s do the math.

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:40.140
Single adults tend to spend between $59 and
$91 every week at the grocery store, so about

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.210
an average of $75 per week.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:47.310
There’s no stats about how much single adults
spend on food delivery, but we do know how

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.050
much Gen Zers spend on food delivery which
should be pretty close.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.280
The average Gen Zer spends about $30 per food
delivery order.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.300
So, even ordering food delivery just once
a week would increase the average person's

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.620
food budget by almost 50%.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.810
Twice a week would increase the budget by
80%, and 3 times a week would increase the

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.550
budget by 120%.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:13.819
For those of you who use food delivery every
day, I know you’re watching, you’re increasing

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:20.430
you’re food budget by nearly 300%, spending
$210 every week on food delivery.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:25.960
On the brightside, your grocery bill is probably
far lower than average, but that’s not exactly

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.500
a net positive.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:32.360
To make things worse, this shortfall will
only compound throughout your life.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:37.980
Just one food delivery per week will set your
retirement back $600,000, obviously assuming

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.730
that you invest your money well instead.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:45.860
This means that 2 deliveries per week is costing
you $1.2 million, 3 delivers is costing you

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:50.560
$1.8 million, and a delivery every day is
costing you $4.2 million.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:56.150
At that point, that could literally be your
entire retirement.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:01.040
The only way that this is not a bad financial
decision is if you’re already investing

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.250
a $100,000 or more every year.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:07.870
At that rate, you’d be worth $40 million
by the time you’re 65.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.650
$4.2 million is still a pretty large amount,
but percentage wise, it’s not terrible.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:18.479
I suspect that most of you aren’t investing
a $100,000 every year though, so probably

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:20.490
best to cut back on the food delivery.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.570
Now, I know what some of you are already saying.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:27.940
What’s the point of having so much money
at retirement if you didn’t even get to

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.230
enjoy the journey there and that is valid.

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:35.920
74% of people say that they would show up
to an event if they knew the food was going

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:41.410
to be good, so there’s no doubt that food
plays a big role in people’s lives.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:46.509
This brings us to exhibit B which is how much
money is going towards the food itself and

00:04:46.509 --> 00:04:48.720
how much is going towards delivery.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.699
It may seem like you’re just paying a delivery
fee and a tip, but that’s just what’s

00:04:53.699 --> 00:04:54.699
shown.

00:04:54.699 --> 00:04:58.160
You see, restaurants know that people are
willing to pay more for the same food when

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.820
it’s offered for delivery, and they’ve
taken the liberty to increase prices across

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.910
the board on delivery apps.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.540
Take Panda Express for example.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:12.910
A family feast costs $39 at the restaurant,
but it costs between $45 and $47 on delivery

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:13.910
apps.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:18.590
It might not seem like a big difference, but
when you combine this with delivery fees and

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:22.030
service fees, it becomes quite egregious.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:29.669
The same Subway order costs 25% more on Grubhub,
46% more on DoorDash, 63% more on Postmates,

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:35.220
and 91% more on Uber Eats, and that not even
accounting for a tip.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:41.641
On average, people tip 15.7% for food deliveries,
so really you’re spending an extra 40 to

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:45.310
100% over your meal price just for the delivery.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:48.120
Let’s call it an average of 70%.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:53.910
Here’s what this means long term. $247,000
of this is going to delivery.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:58.130
$494,000 of this is going to delivery.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:01.560
$741,000 of this is going to delivery.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.800
And a disgusting $1.7 million of this is going
to delivery.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:12.099
That’s enough money to make a grown man
cry, but all of that is just the consumer

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:15.699
side of things.

00:06:15.699 --> 00:06:22.280
SCAMMING DRIVERS:
YouTube has glamorized the life of delivery

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.280
drivers.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.370
Trying to make $500 in a day.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:27.400
Trying to make $700 in a day.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.259
Trying to make $10,000 in a month.

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:32.060
I mean, there’s nothing wrong with these
videos.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:37.440
There fun entertaining challenges, but don’t
take them for anything more than just that.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.340
While delivering food may be a lucrative avenue
for broke college students and young adults,

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:45.370
it’s not exactly as good as it sounds especially
as a career.

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:51.139
To reach numbers like $10,000 per month, delivery
drivers have to work 12 to 15 hours every

00:06:51.139 --> 00:06:52.400
single day.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.879
And when you do the math, it’s not all that
great.

00:06:55.879 --> 00:07:00.750
Even calling it 12 hours per day, delivery
drivers have to work 360 hours per month to

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.300
reach those numbers.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:06.949
If delivery drivers worked a more reasonable
40 hours per week or a 160 hours per month,

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:12.930
they’d make about $53,000 per year which
doesn’t sound too bad, but that number is

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:17.460
actually super misleading because most of
us contextualize that number as a job.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:22.290
I mean, that’s how much teachers and police
officers make, but the difference between

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:27.160
making $53,000 on Uber Eats and making $53,000
at a job is night and day.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:32.710
First of all, the job is infinitely more stable,
but let’s put that aside as there’s plenty

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:34.710
of other attributes to discuss.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:39.300
For example, most of the numbers you see on
YouTube are top line numbers that don’t

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:43.250
account for gas or depreciation of the car,
but let’s even put that aside.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:48.120
The real first obstacle that you’ll face
as a delivery driver is taxes.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:52.220
As a delivery driver, your tax status will
be self employed which means that you’ll

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:55.970
have to pay both sides of social security
and medicare tax.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:08:02.919
This adds up to an extra 7.65% every single
year, so earning $53,000 on Uber Eats is effectively

00:08:02.919 --> 00:08:07.940
the same as earning $49,000 at a job, and
that’s just because of taxes.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.009
Next, you have deal with health insurance.

00:08:11.009 --> 00:08:16.009
Most people making $49,000 per year have some
sort of government job or low end private

00:08:16.009 --> 00:08:17.009
job.

00:08:17.009 --> 00:08:20.310
Either way though, the benefits are pretty
strong in most cases.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:25.000
The average teacher, for example, gets $10,500
in health insurance benefits.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:29.040
As a delivery driver, you’ll have to pay
this amount out of pocket which takes your

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.790
salary down to $38,500.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:34.690
After that, we have retirement benefits.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:39.240
Police officers for example, get as much as
$29,000 towards retirement every year.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:43.800
If you’re trying to match this as a delivery
driver, you’ll be down to less than $10,000

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:44.800
in net income.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.660
But, let’s be more conservative.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:50.790
Let’s say that you’re missing out on $5,000
in retirement benefits.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.220
That still knocks you down to $33,500.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:58.410
Most jobs will also have paid time off, sick
days, and other perks.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:03.320
But, if you want these as a delivery driver,
you’ll have to pay it out of pocket which

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:08.540
will bring your net income to below $30,000,
but it gets even worse.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:13.570
When you hear that police officers and teachers
make $50 or $60,000, that’s truly an average

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:15.140
across the nation.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:20.079
These jobs exist in every community and every
city across the world really.

00:09:20.079 --> 00:09:24.350
The opportunity to do Uber Eats food full
time, however, only exists in highest cost

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:29.060
of living cities in the world like New York,
San Francisco, London, Austin, etc.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:34.180
I mean, you’re not gonna be very busy if
you’re trying to do Uber Eats in Nebraska.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:40.010
In many of these cities, the minimum wage
itself is $15 per hour which translates to

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.079
just over $30,000 per year.

00:09:42.079 --> 00:09:46.600
So, while topline numbers on YouTube challenge
videos may look appealing, driving for these

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:52.250
food delivery apps is basically just an unstable
minimum wage job.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:59.860
SCAMMING RESTAURANTS:
It’s not just consumers and drivers that

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:02.990
are getting screwed over by these apps either.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:05.970
Restaurants are also getting the short end
of the stick.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.810
Have you ever wondered why delivery prices
are relatively affordable?

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:15.040
Of course, we discussed how even these small
$10 or $15 fees can add up over time, but

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.480
in terms of face value, it’s not too big
of a fee to have any restaurant delivered

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:20.480
to your doorstep.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:26.060
Well, the reason for that is that you’re
not the only one that’s paying.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:30.550
Restaurants have to be exorbitant amounts
to be listed on these food delivery apps which

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:32.880
was basically required during the pandemic.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:39.389
On DoorDash, for example, restaurants have
to pay 15, 25, or 30% depending on how much

00:10:39.389 --> 00:10:42.370
they want DoorDash to push their restaurant.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:47.959
DoorDash also gets an additional 6% commission
on pickup orders given that they can’t charge

00:10:47.959 --> 00:10:49.779
you a delivery fee.

00:10:49.779 --> 00:10:54.760
Uber Eats and all of the other delivery apps
have virtually identical pricing plans.

00:10:54.760 --> 00:11:00.029
And given that restaurants tend to have pretty
meager profit margins of 3-5%, there’s no

00:11:00.029 --> 00:11:04.690
question that these fees weigh down these
businesses quite a bit.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:09.050
This is the main reason that restaurants markup
their food so heavily on these delivery apps

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:10.459
in the first place.

00:11:10.459 --> 00:11:15.110
Oftentimes, they have to charge that much
more just to breakeven.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:19.850
They also have no option to avoid these apps
either because the pandemic transitioned much

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:22.310
of the customer base to delivery.

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:26.730
But, delivery apps aren’t just hurting the
bottomline of restaurants, they’re also

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:29.170
oftentimes hurting their reputation.

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:33.769
You see, first these delivery apps use extremely
aggressive salespeople to convince restaurant

00:11:33.769 --> 00:11:35.640
owners to list their restaurants.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:41.480
But, if they don’t agree to do it voluntarily,
these apps often just go ahead and list the

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:42.740
restaurant anyway.

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:47.500
They figure that most owners don’t have
the time, money, or energy to take these corporations

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:48.770
to court.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:53.480
Maybe 1 in a 1000 or 1 in 10,000 will actually
sue and win.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:55.930
But this doesn’t really matter for these
apps.

00:11:55.930 --> 00:12:01.840
They’re basically having to pay $300,000
or $500,000 for every 10,000 restaurants that

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:03.010
they list.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:08.640
So, listing these restaurants without the
owner’s permission is a no brainer net positive.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:11.730
The same, however, cannot be said for owners.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:16.950
When these apps list menus without their permission,
they often end up using outdated menus and

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:17.950
prices.

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:21.970
So, customers end up ordering something and
get mad when they realize that the food is

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:25.610
no longer being offered or costs more than
they thought.

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:29.610
Customers will naturally get mad at the restaurant
and think: why don’t these people even keep

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:35.360
their menus up to date, but in reality, it
wasn’t even the restaurant's fault.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:39.690
These frustrations don’t just apply to wrongfully
listed restaurants either.

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:43.510
Correctly listed restaurants have their own
set of headaches with delivery drivers and

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:44.740
angry customers.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:49.470
For example, drivers have the ability to drop
an order up until they pick it up.

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:52.130
And this happens more often than you think.

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:56.180
The driver arrives at the store and sees that
the line is super long or they find a more

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:59.279
lucrative delivery or whatever the reason
may be.

00:12:59.279 --> 00:13:03.880
If another driver doesn’t accept to do the
delivery, the food is ready and the customers

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:09.250
wants the food, but the food will never get
to the customer, the customer will want a

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:13.279
refund, and restaurant will have lost out
on the food and the money.

00:13:13.279 --> 00:13:18.190
Little to say, there’s not much for restaurants
to get excited about when it comes to food

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:21.610
delivery apps.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:27.080
SCAMMING THEMSELVES:
With customers getting screwed over, drivers

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:32.040
getting screwed over, and restaurants getting
screwed over, you would think that atleast

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:38.070
the food delivery apps must be coming out
on top, but this is also not the case.

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:43.380
All food delivery apps lose money and the
most popular ones lose the most money.

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:47.779
Inflation is only making these losses even
worse with most delivery apps posting record

00:13:47.779 --> 00:13:48.779
losses.

00:13:48.779 --> 00:13:53.230
In the last 12 months, DoorDash lost a record
$1.36 billion.

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:59.490
But, that’s nothing in comparison to Uber’s
$9.1 billion loss.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:05.070
For Uber, this isn’t even a record as they’ve
been casually losing $8 to $10 billion for

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:06.810
4 years now.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:12.139
With all the fees they charge everyone, this
might be a bit confusing, but when you think

00:14:12.139 --> 00:14:14.670
about it, it all makes sense.

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:20.390
The only food industry that has mastered delivery
and is able to stay profitable if pizza chains,

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:23.380
and they had to make a lot of sacrifices to
achieve this.

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:28.120
Every restaurant only delivers to a very small
radius, they have full time delivery drivers,

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:32.670
and they’ve refined their delivery process
to be extremely efficient over decades of

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:33.810
doing this.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:38.170
Modern delivery apps are basically the exact
opposite of this.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:42.050
Drivers usually have to go to different restaurants
for each delivery, they have to deliver to

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:47.019
a very big radius, and drivers generally choose
deliveries based on how lucrative they are

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:50.750
as opposed to how efficient they are for the
overall platform.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:55.880
Given this combination, it’s no wonder why
delivery apps are struggling to turn a profit.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:15:01.180
The bottom line is that delivering from any
restaurant to any doorstep is simply a logistical

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:06.920
nightmare that requires a bunch of man hours
which is not cost effective by any means,

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:08.230
and it never will be.

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:13.079
In the meantime, everyone from drivers and
consumers to restaurants and delivery apps

00:15:13.079 --> 00:15:16.720
will have to put up with the associated expenses.

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:21.491
And to think that all of this pain for everyone
can be traced back to us being too lazy to

00:15:21.491 --> 00:15:25.370
not just cook but even pick up cooked food.

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:27.660
How often do you use food delivery apps?

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:28.720
Comment that down below.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:33.790
Also, drop a like if this video made you look
at food delivery differently.

